course: history
lesson: 53
title: China: the four great inventions
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Mei says China gave the world paper, printing, the compass and gunpowder, the «four great inventions». This time the teacher agrees completely: all four were first developed in China and spread west over centuries.
explain: Open your pencil case: paper in your notebook, printed words on the cover, maybe a compass on your ruler. Then think of New Year fireworks. Each of these began in China.
explain: But Mei sometimes adds too much: gunpowder was not «only for fireworks», Marco Polo did not bring noodles to Italy, and some things, like the crossbow, were invented in more than one place.
explain: China also gave the world porcelain, tea, kites and an early earthquake detector with bronze dragons; a good historian enjoys the true list and checks the extra claims.
fact: Paper, printing, the magnetic compass and gunpowder were all first developed in China and spread west; calling them «the four great inventions» became popular in modern times.
fact: The Diamond Sutra, a Buddhist holy text printed in 868 AD and found in a cave at Dunhuang, is the oldest dated printed book; it is now in the British Library in London.
fact: Bi Sheng made clay movable type around 1040 AD, Korea printed the Jikji with metal type in 1377, and Gutenberg's press of about 1450 used metal type, which suited the alphabet.
fact: Chinese printers often kept using carved woodblocks, because Chinese needs thousands of different characters.
fact: In the Han era Chinese thinkers used magnetic spoons for divination (trying to tell the future), and by the 11th century compasses were used for navigation at sea.
fact: Chinese alchemists, early experimenters who mixed substances, found gunpowder around the 9th century while looking for medicines for long life; it was used for fireworks and also for fire arrows, bombs and early guns.
fact: Porcelain was perfected in China, especially at Jingdezhen; the word «china» for porcelain comes from the country's name, which probably comes from the Qin.
fact: Zhang Heng built a seismoscope in 132 AD: a bronze jar with dragons that dropped a ball into a toad's mouth to show the direction of an earthquake.
fact: Early wheelbarrows are recorded in Han China and crossbows by the 5th century BC; ancient Greece had its own early forms of both.
fact: Tea drinking began in China and had already spread to many lands by trade; in the 1840s the Briton Robert Fortune secretly took tea plants and know-how from China to India.
fact: A 4,000-year-old bowl of millet noodles was found at Lajia in China; Italy already had pasta before Marco Polo, and the story that he brought noodles home comes from a 1929 American magazine.
fact: Kites were flown in China at least by the 5th century BC and used for signals; sunshades and umbrellas were also known in ancient Egypt and Assyria.
exercise: Which are the four great inventions? | Paper, printing, the compass and gunpowder | Silk, tea, rice and kites | The wheel, the plough, the glass window and the bar of soap | Chess, football, pasta and ice cream
exercise: What is the oldest dated printed book? | The Diamond Sutra of 868 AD | Gutenberg's Bible from Mainz | The Jikji of 1377 | The Art of War, by Sun Tzu himself
exercise: Where was the Diamond Sutra found? | In a cave at Dunhuang | Under the Great Wall at Shanhaiguan | In a Roman temple in Italy | In Marco Polo's house in Venice
exercise: Where is the Diamond Sutra kept today? | The British Library in London | The Forbidden City in Beijing | The Louvre in Paris | A cave in Japan
exercise: Who made clay movable type around 1040 AD? | Bi Sheng | Gutenberg | Cai Lun of the Han | Confucius
exercise: Why did Chinese printers often keep using woodblocks? | Chinese needs thousands of characters | They had no wood left in China to make type | Movable type was banned | Ink did not stick to type
exercise: What was the compass first used for in China? | Divination | Driving cars | Telling the time | Weighing rice
exercise: By when were compasses used for navigation at sea? | By the 11th century | By 5000 BC | Only after 1900 | Never in China, only in Europe
exercise: What were the alchemists looking for when they found gunpowder? | Medicines for long life | A way to make gold rain | A new kind of tea | A cure for hiccups only
exercise: How was gunpowder used in China? | In fireworks and in weapons | Only in fireworks | Only to cook rice | Only to light the lamps in temples
exercise: Which city is famous for porcelain? | Jingdezhen | Venice in Italy | Kyiv in Ukraine | Timbuktu in Mali
exercise: What did the dragons on Zhang Heng's jar do? | Dropped a ball into a toad's mouth | Breathed real fire | Sang a little song whenever it rained | Spun like a compass
exercise: What did Zhang Heng's seismoscope show? | The direction of an earthquake | How strong a storm was | What time it was | Where gold was buried
exercise: Who took tea plants from China to India in the 1840s? | Robert Fortune | Marco Polo of Venice | The Jesuit Matteo Ricci | Admiral Zheng He
exercise: How old is the noodle bowl found at Lajia? | About 4,000 years | About 40 years | About 400 years | About 40,000 years
exercise: What were early Chinese kites used for? | Signals | Carrying letters to the Moon | Catching fish | Making rain
test: Mei says China invented paper, printing, the compass and gunpowder. What does the teacher say? | He agrees; this one is true | He says Europe invented them all | He says Egypt invented them all | He says nobody knows
test: The teacher says «Gutenberg invented printing». Mei objects. Who is right? | Mei; China printed earlier, Gutenberg made an important version | The teacher; nobody anywhere printed a single page before Gutenberg did | Neither; printing came in 1900 | Both wrong; the Romans did
test: mei reckons china only ever used gunpowder for fireworks. right? | No, it was also used in weapons | Yes, only fireworks | Yes, only for cooking | No, only for medicine
test: Nonna says Marco Polo brought spaghetti from China. Fix it kindly. | Italy had pasta before him; the story is a 20th-century myth | She is right; it is proven by the letters he wrote home from China | Spaghetti came from Mexico | Marco Polo invented noodles
test: Mei says English named porcelain «china» after her country. What came first? | The country's name; porcelain was named after it | The porcelain; the country later took its name from the cups | The tea | Neither word is old
test: The teacher names Greek crossbows, Mei names Chinese ones. Can both be right? | Yes, some things were invented in more than one place | No, only one place can ever invent anything, so one of them copied | Only the Greeks | Only the Chinese
test: What would happen if a printer tried to make movable type for all Chinese characters? | He would need thousands of different pieces | He would need only 26 | He would need none | He would need only 10
test: A cartoon shows Zhang Heng's machine measuring how strong an earthquake was. What is wrong? | It showed the direction, not the strength | It measured the weather | It showed nothing at all | Nothing is wrong
test: «English just stole tea» says Mei, dead angry. What do historians say? | Fortune smuggled plants out, but tea had already spread by trade | Tea was invented in England and taken to China by British sailors | Nobody took anything | Tea came from Brazil
test: Mei says China invented both the kite and the umbrella. Which claim is stronger? | The kite; umbrellas have several ancient homes | The umbrella; kites came from Rome | Both are false | Both are certain
test: Why did the alphabet suit Gutenberg's movable type so well? | It has only a few dozen letters to make | Letters are prettier | Europe had no wood | The alphabet was Chinese
exam: Identify the four inventions conventionally designated the four great inventions of China. | Paper, printing, the magnetic compass and gunpowder | Silk, porcelain, tea and the kite | The wheel, the plough, glass and the steam engine | Chess, pasta, ice cream and football
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the earliest dated printed book. | The Diamond Sutra, printed in 868 and recovered at Dunhuang | The Bible printed by Gutenberg in the city of Mainz circa 1450 | The Jikji, printed with metal type in 1377 | A Roman almanac of the first century
exam: Ascertain the purpose pursued by those alchemists to whom the discovery of gunpowder is attributed. | The preparation of medicines conferring long life | The manufacture of porcelain | The navigation of ships at sea | The detection of earthquakes
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the belief that Marco Polo introduced noodles into Italy? | It is a modern myth, pasta having been known in Italy previously | It is conclusively established by his writings and by the records of Venice | It is attested by the Diamond Sutra | It is confirmed by the bowl found at Lajia
